\chapter{Running weft}
\label{ch:running}

weft is a command-line tool. This chapter covers how it is invoked, how
to choose between tangling and weaving, the three woven formats, and the
full set of flags. (The introspection flags --- \verb|-m|, \verb|-e|,
\verb|-R|, \verb|--lint|, and friends --- have their own chapter,
Chapter~\ref{ch:ai}.)

\section{Invocation and defaults}

\begin{quote}
{\tt weft} \textit{flags file}\ldots
\end{quote}

\noindent One or more files may be processed at once. A file name with no
extension gets \verb|.weft| appended. The input may live in another
directory, but the woven document is always written to the current
directory. So \verb|weft /foo/bar/quux| reads \verb|/foo/bar/quux.weft|
and, by default, writes only the tangled output files.

That default is the important part: \textbf{weft only tangles unless you
ask for a document.} It generates the files named by \verb|@@o| and
nothing else, so it drops quietly into a normal build. Weaving happens
only with \verb|-w|, and then tangling and weaving run in a single pass.

Output files are written to \emph{existing} directories: a subdirectory in
an \verb|@@o| name (\verb|@@o src/gen/foo.c|) or a \verb|-p| prefix whose
directory is missing is an error --- the strict default catches typos.
Pass \verb|--mkdirs| to have weft create those directories itself
(\verb|mkdir -p|) before writing.

\section{Choosing what to produce}

Three flags select the work:

\begin{quote}
\begin{tabular}{@@{}ll@@{}}
\verb|-t| & Suppress weaving (override a \verb|-w| in a script). \\
\verb|-o| & Suppress tangling --- weave only. \\
\verb|-c| & Skip the change-test before overwriting output files. \\
\end{tabular}
\end{quote}

\noindent By default weft writes each output file to a temporary location
and compares it with the existing file, replacing it only if it changed
--- so \verb|make| does not rebuild untouched files. \verb|-c| skips that
comparison. As an extreme, \verb|weft -to file| scans the input and
produces nothing at all.

\section{Weaving to \LaTeX}

\verb|weft -w tex file.weft| produces \verb|file.tex|. Because scrap
numbers depend on where things land on the page, they cannot be known
until \LaTeX\ has run; weft resolves them by reading the \verb|.aux| file
on a \emph{second} pass. The usual recipe is therefore:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
weft -w tex file.weft && pdflatex file.tex && \
weft -w tex file.weft && pdflatex file.tex
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\noindent weft warns when a rerun is needed; if it cannot tell, \LaTeX\
warns that labels may have changed. Add \verb|-r| to emit hyperlinks
(your preamble must load \verb|hyperref|), and \verb|-n| to number scraps
1, 2, 3\ldots\ sequentially instead of by page --- handy for small webs.

\section{Weaving to Markdown}

\verb|weft -w md file.weft| produces \verb|file.md|, readable on any
platform that renders Markdown --- GitHub, GitLab, VS~Code --- with no
\TeX\ installation. It keeps the essentials (scrap definitions,
cross-references, indices) with Markdown-native conventions:

\begin{description}
\item[Headers] Files as \verb|**"name"** **N** =|, fragments as
\verb|**<*Name* N>** =|.
\item[Bodies] Code in \verb|<pre><code>| blocks (not fenced), so
references inside code can be clickable links.
\item[Cross-references] Each scrap has an HTML anchor \verb|<a id="weftN">|
and links use \verb|<a href="#weftN">|; footers are blockquotes.
\item[Numbering] Sequential (\verb|-n| is forced --- Markdown has no
pages), no letter suffixes.
\item[Highlighting] \verb|<code class="language-X">| when the language is
known (Chapter~\ref{ch:languages}).
\item[Fences] Cosmetic triple-backtick fences in the source are silently
stripped.
\end{description}

\noindent Tangling is identical regardless of format; with \verb|-w| a
single run produces both the document and the output files.

\section{Weaving to HTML}

\verb|weft -w html file.weft| produces \verb|file.html|: a complete,
self-contained web page --- doctype, an embedded style sheet, and body ---
that opens in any browser with no external tooling, no \TeX, and no
Markdown processor. It is the quickest way to a browsable, cross-referenced
rendering of a web.

\begin{description}
\item[Self-contained] The generated \verb|.html| carries its own CSS
(fragment names in an accent colour, soft code blocks, accent links) and
adapts to the reader's light or dark theme.
\item[Prose is HTML] As with every weave, the prose is copied through
verbatim, so in an HTML web you write HTML prose (\verb|<h1>|, \verb|<p>|,
\ldots) around your scraps --- just as \verb|-w tex| expects \LaTeX\ and
\verb|-w md| expects Markdown.
\item[Headers] Fragment and file definitions render as
\hbox{$\langle$\,\textit{Name} N\,$\rangle$} with the
\(\equiv\)/\(+\!\equiv\) definition marker.
\item[Cross-references] A fragment reference in the prose becomes a
clickable link to its definition; scrap footers (``used in'', ``also
at'') and the \verb|@@f|/\verb|@@m|/\verb|@@u| indices are clickable too,
via \verb|#weftN| anchors within the page.
\item[Numbering] Sequential (like Markdown --- a page has no page numbers).
\item[Highlighting] \verb|<code class="language-X">| when the language is
known (Chapter~\ref{ch:languages}).
\end{description}

\noindent The format can also be declared in the source with
\verb|@@W html| and then invoked with a bare \verb|-w|.

\section{All command-line flags}

\begin{quote}
\begin{tabular}{@@{}ll@@{}}
\verb|-w| {\rm[}\textit{fmt}{\rm]} & Weave. \verb|-w tex|, \verb|-w md|, \verb|-w html|, or \verb|-w| alone (uses \verb|@@W|). \\
\verb|-t| & Suppress weaving. \\
\verb|-o| & Suppress tangling. \\
\verb|-c| & Do not test output files for change before writing. \\
\verb|-p| \textit{path} & Prepend \textit{path} to every output file name. \\
\verb|--mkdirs| & Create missing output directories (\verb|mkdir -p|). \\
\verb|-V| \textit{str} & Value for \verb|@@v| (defaults to weft's version). \\
\verb|-n| & Number scraps sequentially instead of by page. \\
\verb|-r| & Emit hyperlinks (needs \verb|hyperref|). \\
\verb|-x| & Include cross-reference numbers in scrap comments. \\
\verb|-h| \textit{opts} & Options for the \verb|hyperref| package. \\
\verb|-v| & Verbose progress on \verb|stderr|. \\
\verb|-s| & Do not list the scraps that make up each file. \\
\verb|-d| & Print dangling identifiers (never referenced). \\
\verb|--help| & Full usage, a directives summary, and the AI workflow. \\
\end{tabular}
\end{quote}

\noindent For the project-map, extraction, reverse-map, lint, diff, and
error-translation flags, see Chapter~\ref{ch:ai}.
